    "q-143486": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143486",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ bushes is drawn from a population. The population has bushes of two different heights, $4\\,\\text{ft}$ and $5\\,\\text{ft},$ distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.01\\,\\text{ft}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bushes in the population have heights $4\\,\\text{ft}$ and $5\\,\\text{ft},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+2=5$ bushes only with the following heights:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\text{ft},\\,4\\,\\text{ft},\\,4\\,\\text{ft},\\,5\\,\\text{ft},\\,5\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(12+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{5} \\\\  & =4.4\\,\\text{ft},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot {4}^{2}+2\\cdot {5}^{2})-{4.4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(98)-19.36 \\\\  & =19.6-19.36 \\\\  & =0.24\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.24},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.01\\,\\text{ft}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{n}} & <0.01 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.24}}{0.01} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{{0.01}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{2\\,400} \\\\ n & >2\\,400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=2\\,401.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
